N7, "Agents That Work Together": no book has it as its title. Open Library: 0 titles. A web search limited to amazon.com and amazon.co.uk found no exact title, but four Kindle books use the words inside a longer title or subtitle, all from 2026:
- "Multi-Agent Systems for Developers: Orchestrating Teams of AI Agents That Work Together" (Jacob Ren, August 2026)
- "Multi-Agent AI: When AI Agents Work Together" (September 2026)
- "Build Your AI Agent Team: ... Specialized AI Agents That Work Together" (Michael A. Collins)
- "Multi-Agent AI Systems Explained: How to Make AI Agents Work Together on Complex Tasks" (Jake Morrison)
So the title is free, but the phrase is in use in the multi-agent how-to shelf. Searching on it will put this book beside those.

N8, "Brakes So We Can Go Faster": no book, and no page anywhere with it as a title. Open Library: 0 titles. The web search finds only the idea it comes from: motorsport and mountain-biking pieces saying good brakes let you go faster. Nobody owns the phrase, and the image is a familiar one.

What I could not reach:
- Amazon's own search answered the container with a robot check (HTTP 202), so these come from a search engine's index of Amazon, not Amazon's live catalogue.
- Google Books refused for rate (HTTP 429).
- The UK ISBN agency (Nielsen) has no public title search; its database is behind an account.
A last exact-title check in Amazon's store, by a person, before the editor signs, would close it. The publisher can also ask Nielsen when the ISBN is bought.

Nothing here reaches the register: no number will print.

